funcdemo1.asm: basics of call and ret with no parameters
funcdemo2.asm: simple function with 2 parameters and a return value compare
	and contrast old way and new way.
funcdemo3.asm: long calc(long a, long b, long c, long d, long e, long f, long g, long h)
funcdemo4.asm: recursive nfact() (old way and new way)
funcdemo5.asm: recursive function without tail recursion

------------------------------------------------------------
User Program Calling Conventions
---------------------------------
rdi -> param #1
rsi -> param #2
rdx -> param #3
rcx -> param #4
r8 -> param #5
r9 -> param #6
Parameters 7, 8, ... all are placed on the stack (reverse order so that
		when they are popped off they will come off in order).

rax, rdx  <- hold return value of function

Floating point parameters go in xmm0, xmm1, xmm2, ...
xmm0, xmm1 <- hold floating point return values



------------------------------------------------------------
Linux System Call Calling Conventions
--------------------------------------
rax -> system call number
rdi -> param #1
rsi -> param #2
rdx -> param #3
r10 -> param #4
r8 -> param #5
r9 -> param #6

rax, rdx  <- hold return value of function

Floating point parameters go in xmm0, xmm1, xmm2, ...
xmm0, xmm1 <- hold floating point return values

NOTE: variable-argument subroutines require a value in rax for
   the number of vector registers used

NOTE: registers rbp, rbx, r12-r15 will not be affected by system calls

